ready: 1
kind: rule
id: recall.rule.render
display_id: RULE-RECALL-RENDER
module: recall
title: How memory appears in the agent's context
summary: Memory is shown as dated, labeled notes under a line saying they are not instructions, and a checkpoint is shown as where the agent left off.
when: Kernle places any memory in an agent's context, through load, the session index or a cue
then: >-
  The block starts with the injection marker and the preamble; each memory is a
  quoted note with its kind, trust tier and date; the latest checkpoint appears
  under "Where you left off" with its task, context and next steps and no
  instruction about how to resume.
constraints:
  - text: No rendered block tells the agent to act, to continue without asking, or to hide that memory was loaded.
    hard: true
  - text: Content written by third-party sources carries its trust tier on every line it appears.
    hard: true
constants:
  - recall.const.injection-preamble
  - recall.const.injection-marker
rationale: >-
  An agent that treats memory as orders can be steered by whatever got into
  memory, including text planted by a web page or a repository file. Labeled
  notes with kind, trust tier and date let it weigh them like a colleague's
  advice. The marker lets capture drop Kernle's own output so recall never
  reinforces itself.
